  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Between Gourdon, Le Bar-sur-Loup and Tourettes-sur-Loup, these gorges are known for their waterfalls and fast-flowing water, in a spectacular setting in the Nice backcountry, well away from the coast's beach atmosphere. The Cascade de Courmes has been a listed historic site since 1913.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>A major safety point: swimming is officially and explicitly BANNED at the Cascade de Courmes, under a municipal order of 8 April 2003 that bans all sporting activity and swimming across the whole site (listed site, DREAL regulatory record). At the Saut du Loup too, entering the water is reported as strictly banned by a municipal sign. Access to the banks is largely private — only the path itself is public. Dogs are tolerated provided they are kept on a leash (Préalpes d'Azur Regional Nature Park). No parking is possible right at the Cascade de Courmes (roadside only); a car park exists at the Saut du Loup, along the gorge road at Gourdon.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the perched village of Gourdon, clinging to the cliff, offers a dizzying view over the gorges — a great stop to admire the site, but plan for a walk, not a swim, which is strictly banned here.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Saut du Loup, an iconic waterfall in the area, is a classic stop for visitors to the region (swimming also banned, see above).</p>
    </div>

